  { n:34, summary:"Josiah was eight years old when he began to reign and ruled thirty-one years, doing right and walking in David's ways. In the eighth year he began to seek the God of David his father; in the twelfth he began purging Judah and Jerusalem of high places, groves, and images. In his eighteenth year he sent Shaphan the scribe to repair the house of the LORD, and the workmen dealt faithfully. Then Hilkiah the high priest found a book of the law of the LORD given by Moses and delivered it to Shaphan, who read it before the king. Josiah rent his clothes and sent men to inquire of the LORD. Huldah the prophetess answered: evil was coming on the place, but because the king's heart was tender and he humbled himself and wept, he would be gathered to his grave in peace. Josiah gathered all Judah, read the covenant book aloud, and made all present covenant to follow the LORD with all their heart; all his days they departed not from following him.",
    context:"Josiah's temple repairs uncover the book of the law, leading to covenant renewal under Huldah's word.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"2 Chronicles 34:14", text:"And when they brought out the money that was brought into the house of the LORD, Hilkiah the priest found a book of the law of the LORD given by Moses." },
      { ref:"2 Chronicles 34:19", text:"And it came to pass, when the king had heard the words of the law, that he rent his clothes." },
      { ref:"2 Chronicles 34:27", text:"Because thine heart was tender, and thou didst humble thyself before God, when thou heardest his words against this place, and against the inhabitants thereof, and humbledst thyself before me, and didst rend thy clothes, and weep before me; I have even heard thee also, saith the LORD." }
    ],
    takeaway:"A tender, humble heart before God's word turns even announced judgment toward peace.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Who found the book of the law in the house of the LORD?", choices:["Shaphan the scribe","Huldah's husband","Hilkiah the high priest","Joah the recorder"], a:2, why:"When they brought out the money... Hilkiah the priest found a book of the law of the LORD given by Moses." },
      { q:"How did Josiah react to hearing the words of the law?", choices:["He disputed its authenticity","He imprisoned Hilkiah","He ordered the book hidden","He rent his clothes and sent men to inquire of the LORD"], a:3, why:"When the king had heard the words of the law, that he rent his clothes, for great was the wrath of the LORD poured out upon them." },
      { q:"Why did the LORD soften judgment toward Josiah personally?", choices:["Because his heart was tender and he humbled himself when he heard God's words","He had rebuilt the temple walls","He was too young to be guilty","Huldah interceded for him"], a:0, why:"Because thine heart was tender, and thou didst humble thyself before God... I have even heard thee also, saith the LORD." }
    ] },
  { n:35, summary:"Josiah kept a passover to the LORD in Jerusalem on the fourteenth day of the first month. He set the priests in their charges, told the Levites to put the holy ark in the house Solomon built so it would no more be a burden on their shoulders, and organized the courses by the writings of David and Solomon. From his own substance he gave the people thirty thousand lambs and kids and three thousand bullocks, and princes and chief Levites gave thousands more. Priests sprinkled the blood, Levites flayed the offerings, and singers and porters kept their posts. There was no passover like it, the chapter says, from the days of Samuel the prophet — and it fell in Josiah's eighteenth year. Then Necho king of Egypt marched toward Carchemish by Euphrates; Josiah met him in the valley of Megiddo, refusing a warning spoken from the mouth of God. Archers shot the king, he died in Jerusalem, and Jeremiah lamented him.",
    context:"Josiah's unequaled passover is followed by his death at Megiddo fighting Egypt's Necho.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"2 Chronicles 35:3", text:"And said unto the Levites that taught all Israel, which were holy unto the LORD, Put the holy ark in the house which Solomon the son of David king of Israel did build; it shall not be a burden upon your shoulders: serve now the LORD your God, and his people Israel," },
      { ref:"2 Chronicles 35:18", text:"And there was no passover like to that kept in Israel from the days of Samuel the prophet; neither did all the kings of Israel keep such a passover as Josiah kept, and the priests, and the Levites, and all Judah and Israel that were present, and the inhabitants of Jerusalem." },
      { ref:"2 Chronicles 35:22", text:"Nevertheless Josiah would not turn his face from him, but disguised himself, that he might fight with him, and hearkened not unto the words of Necho from the mouth of God, and came to fight in the valley of Megiddo." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Even the faithful must keep listening; ignoring God's warning can cost what devotion built.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"How did Josiah die?", choices:["Illness in his palace","A conspiracy at Lachish","Captivity in Babylon","Archers shot him at Megiddo"], a:3, why:"The archers shot at king Josiah; he was brought to Jerusalem and died, and all Judah and Jerusalem mourned for him." },
      { q:"What warning did Josiah disregard at Megiddo?", choices:["Necho's message that God commanded his haste — words from the mouth of God","Jeremiah's letter","Huldah's prophecy","A dream the night before"], a:0, why:"He hearkened not unto the words of Necho from the mouth of God, and came to fight in the valley of Megiddo." },
      { q:"What does the chapter pair in Josiah's final record?", choices:["Wealth and war","A matchless passover and a death for not heeding God's voice","Temple gold and tribute","Reform and exile"], a:1, why:"Right after the greatest passover since Samuel, Josiah would not turn his face from Necho and paid with his life." }
    ] },
  { n:36, summary:"The people of the land made Jehoahaz king, but Necho of Egypt deposed him in three months, fined the land a hundred talents of silver and a talent of gold, set his brother Eliakim on the throne as Jehoiakim, and carried Jehoahaz to Egypt. Jehoiakim reigned eleven evil years until Nebuchadnezzar bound him for Babylon. His son Jehoiachin lasted three months and ten days before Babylon took him with the goodly vessels of the temple, making Zedekiah king. Zedekiah reigned eleven years, humbled not himself before Jeremiah, and rebelled against Nebuchadnezzar despite his oath by God. The LORD, having compassion, sent messenger after messenger; but they mocked the messengers of God and misused his prophets, until wrath arose with no remedy. The Chaldees slew the young, burned the house of God, broke Jerusalem's wall, and carried survivors to Babylon, where the land kept sabbath seventy years, fulfilling Jeremiah's word. Then the LORD stirred Cyrus of Persia to proclaim that God had charged him to build a house in Jerusalem: who is among his people? let him go up.",
    context:"Four swift kings end Judah; Babylon burns the temple until Cyrus decrees its rebuilding.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"2 Chronicles 36:16", text:"But they mocked the messengers of God, and despised his words, and misused his prophets, until the wrath of the LORD arose against his people, till there was no remedy." },
      { ref:"2 Chronicles 36:21", text:"To fulfil the word of the LORD by the mouth of Jeremiah, until the land had enjoyed her sabbaths: for as long as she lay desolate she kept sabbath, to fulfil threescore and ten years." },
      { ref:"2 Chronicles 36:23", text:"Thus saith Cyrus king of Persia, All the kingdoms of the earth hath the LORD God of heaven given me; and he hath charged me to build him an house in Jerusalem, which is in Judah. Who is there among you of all his people? The LORD his God be with him, and let him go up." }
    ],
    takeaway:"God's warnings refused bring judgment, yet His word of restoration always stands.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Who issued the decree allowing the exiles to return and rebuild the temple?", choices:["Cyrus king of Persia","Nebuchadnezzar","Darius","Necho"], a:0, why:"In the first year of Cyrus king of Persia the LORD stirred up his spirit, and he made a proclamation that God had charged him to build a house in Jerusalem." },
      { q:"Why did judgment finally fall with no remedy?", choices:["Jeremiah stopped prophesying","They mocked God's messengers, despised His words, and misused His prophets","The temple had no treasure left","Egypt broke its alliance"], a:1, why:"They mocked the messengers of God, and despised his words, and misused his prophets, until the wrath of the LORD arose... till there was no remedy." },
      { q:"How long did the desolate land keep her sabbaths?", choices:["Forty years","Ten years","Seventy years","A thousand years"], a:2, why:"As long as she lay desolate she kept sabbath, to fulfil threescore and ten years, according to Jeremiah's word." }
    ] }
